<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · 1275 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Cantigas de Santa Maria (Canticles of Holy Mary)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="gl">Cantigas de Santa Maria</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A book of 420 songs about the Virgin Mary made at the court of a Spanish king in the 1200s, with pictures of musicians playing.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>poets of the court of Alfonso X of Castile, traditionally all attributed to Alfonso X (possibly Airas Nunes for many; Alfonso for a few)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>musicians of the court of Alfonso X of Castile (not known by name)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the court of Alfonso X of Castile</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Galician-Portuguese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>court of Alfonso X, Kingdom of Castile, present-day Spain</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>during the reign of Alfonso X (d. 1284); codices from perhaps the 1270s to 1284</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by poets and musicians of the court of Alfonso X of Castile (13th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria</a> (&quot;Canticles of Holy Mary&quot;) are 420 poems with musical notation, written in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galician-portuguese">Galician-Portuguese</a> at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a>, called El Sabio, &quot;the Wise&quot; (1221-1284). Tradition attributes them all to Alfonso, but scholars have established that the poets and musicians of his court wrote most of them, with the king credited with a few: he names himself in the first person, and the collection's prologue supports his part. Several studies suggest that the Galician poet Airas Nunes may have written a large number. It is one of the largest collections of monophonic (solo) songs to survive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p>Alfonso's workshops wrote their histories and other prose in Castilian, but the Cantigas are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galician-portuguese">Galician-Portuguese</a>, which was then fashionable as a language of lyric poetry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>; Alfonso had also spent part of his early years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and probably spoke it from childhood. The collection reflects the popularity at his court of other Galician-Portuguese poetry, the cantigas d'amigo and cantigas d'amor.</p><p>Every song mentions the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. Of the 420, 356 are narratives of Marian miracles; the others, apart from an introduction and two prologues, are songs of praise or for Marian feasts, and every tenth song is a hymn. The miracle songs show Mary in a very human way, often taking part in earthly episodes, and many speak of devotion to her in the language of the troubadours' courtly love. The verse forms are extraordinarily varied, 280 different formats for 420 songs, most often the virelai and the rondeau, with lines of two to 24 syllables. The music is written in a notation like that of chant but with some indication of note lengths; its rhythm remains a lively subject of debate.</p><p>The songs survive in four codices, thought to have been made in Alfonso's lifetime: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> codex (To, now in the National Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>), the earliest, with 129 songs, perhaps from the 1270s; the &quot;codex of the musicians&quot; (E, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">El Escorial</a>), the fullest, with 406 songs and miniatures of pairs of musicians playing a wide variety of instruments; the richly illustrated Códice Rico (T, El Escorial), with 195 surviving songs, each shown in six or twelve narrative pictures; and its unfinished sister volume in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> (F), with 111, whose staves were never filled with music. T, F and E date from the early 1280s up to Alfonso's death in 1284.</p><p>The Cantigas are often performed and recorded by early-music groups, among them Eduardo Paniagua and Música Antigua, Federico Bardazzi and Ensemble San Felice, Esther Lamandier and Jordi Savall. Because the original notation is ambiguous, their recordings differ greatly from one another.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The collection is a royal book of devotion to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. Most of its songs tell short stories of miracles in which Mary helps people in everyday troubles, while the rest praise her or celebrate her feasts. They speak of loving her in the manner of a courtly lover serving a lady, and their mood ranges from wonder to joyful praise.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Galician-Portuguese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Galician-Portuguese</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Rosa das rosas e Fror das frores,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rose of roses and Flower of flowers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Dona das donas, Sennor das sennores.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lady of ladies, Lord of lords.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Rosa de beldad' e de parecer</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rose of beauty and of fair appearance</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">e Fror d'alegria e de prazer,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and Flower of joy and of delight,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Dona en mui piadosa seer,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lady in being most merciful,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Sennor en toller coitas e doores.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord in taking away sorrows and pains.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Rosa das rosas e Fror das frores...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rose of roses and Flower of flowers...</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Atal Sennor dev' ome muit' amar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Such a Lord a man must greatly love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">que de todo mal o pode guardar;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who can guard him from all evil;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">e pode-ll' os peccados perdõar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and can pardon him the sins</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">que faz no mundo per maos sabores.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that he commits in the world through evil desires.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Rosa das rosas e Fror das frores...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rose of roses and Flower of flowers...</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Devemo-la muit' amar e servir,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We must greatly love and serve her,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">ca punna de nos guardar de falir;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for she strives to keep us from going astray;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">des i dos erros nos faz repentir,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and also makes us repent of the wrongs</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">que nos fazemos come pecadores.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that we commit as sinners.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Rosa das rosas e Fror das frores...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rose of roses and Flower of flowers...</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Esta dona que tenno por Sennor</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This lady whom I hold as Lord</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">e de que quero seer trobador,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and whose troubadour I wish to be,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">se eu per ren poss' aver seu amor,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if I can by any means have her love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">dou ao demo os outros amores.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I give the other loves to the devil.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gl">Rosa das rosas e Fror das frores...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rose of roses and Flower of flowers...</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Cantigas de Santa Maria (court of Alfonso X, 1252–1284): Cantiga 10, Rosa das rosas. Text from <a href="https://gl.wikisource.org/wiki/Cantiga_X_%3A_Rosa_das_rosas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>line 1.2 and throughout: 'Sennor' (later 'senhor') is the form the troubadour lyric uses for the beloved lady, as her feudal lord; it is kept as 'Lord' because the song sets 'Dona' (Lady) and 'Sennor' side by side. 'Lady of ladies, Lady of lords' is a possible reading of the second half.</li><li>line 2.1: 'parecer' is appearance, looks; 'beldad' beauty.</li><li>line 2.3-4: literally 'Lady in being very merciful, / Lord in removing griefs and pains'; 'coitas' are sorrows, troubles or the pains of love.</li><li>line 3.4: 'per maos sabores' is literally 'through bad tastes': evil appetites, sinful pleasures.</li><li>line 4.2: 'punna' is 'strives, makes an effort'; 'falir' is to fail, err or fall into sin.</li><li>line 4.3: 'des i' is 'moreover, and then'.</li><li>line 5.3: 'per ren' is 'by any thing, by any means'.</li><li>The repeated refrain line ('Rosa das rosas e Fror das frores...') stands for the whole two-line refrain in the source; it is translated as it stands, with its dots.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
